2) Understanding class definitions

Objects and classes consist of attributes (storing the state) and methods (storing the behavior).

class MyCounter:
  def set_count(self, n):
    self.n = n
mc = MyCounter()
mc.set_count(5)
print(mc.n)
=> Note: Notice how you first created a MyCounter class and used self.n to refer to the n attribute inside the class definition, then created a mc object, which allowed you to assign a value of 5 to the object's n attribute using the .set_count() method.

